  "question": {
    "text": "Given a real valued function ' $f$ ' such that\n$f(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{array}{cc}\\frac{\\tan ^2\\{x\\}}{x^2-[x]^2} & \\text { for } x\\gt0 \\\\ 1 & \\text { for } x=0 \\\\ \\sqrt{\\{x\\} \\cot \\{x\\}} & \\text { for } x \\lt 0\\end{array}\\right.$\nthen",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "mg845",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{LHL}=1$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ObhIO",
      "text": "$\\mathrm{RHL}=\\sqrt{\\cot 1}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "bFC27",
      "text": "$\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0} f(x)$ exist",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "wJ3Uc",
      "text": "$\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0} f(x)$ does not exists",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0^{-}} f(x)=\\lim _{h \\rightarrow 0} \\sqrt{\\{-h\\} \\cot \\{-h\\}}$\n$=\\lim _{h \\rightarrow 0} \\sqrt{(1-h) \\cot (1-h)}=\\sqrt{\\cot 1}$\n$\\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0^{+}} f(x)=\\lim _{h \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{\\tan ^2\\{h\\}}{h^2-[h]^2}=\\lim _{h \\rightarrow 0} \\frac{\\tan ^2 h}{h^2}=1$\n$\\therefore \\lim _{x \\rightarrow 0} f(x)$ does not exist.",
    "image": null
  },
